= Dunning–Kruger effect =

== Overview ==

{{:Dunning–Kruger effect/Simple}}

The '''Dunning–Kruger effect''' is a research hypothesis and reported pattern concerning the relationship between objective task performance and the accuracy of self-evaluation. Its common interpretation invokes a metacognitive disadvantage among weaker performers, but the underlying empirical pattern can also be influenced by error in test scores, regression toward the mean, scale boundaries, group stratification and general self-enhancement. The interpretation remains contested.

== What the evidence does and does not establish ==

Kruger and Dunning (1999) reported discrepancies between measured performance and self-assessment in several task domains and proposed a metacognitive explanation. Later studies challenged whether conventional quartile plots isolate deficits in self-insight rather than statistical phenomena. Some subsequent work supports metacognitive accounts under different measurements. These claims must be compared on actual data and test design, not reduced to the popular confidence-versus-experience caricature.<ref name="original_core">Justin Kruger and David Dunning, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.77.6.1121 “Unskilled and unaware of it”], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 77(6), 1121–1134 (1999).</ref><ref name="crit_core">Gilles E. Gignac and Marcin Zajenkowski, [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intell.2020.101449 “The Dunning–Kruger effect is (mostly) a statistical artefact”], ''Intelligence'' 80, 101449 (2020).</ref><ref name="reply_core">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intell.2022.101717 “Reevaluating the Dunning-Kruger effect: A response to and replication of Gignac and Zajenkowski (2020)”], ''Intelligence'' (2022), illustration of contested interpretations.</ref>

== Measurement requirements ==

A valid study needs a reliable ability measure, an independent self-evaluation measure, consistent scales and appropriate controls for regression and ceiling/floor effects. Rank-based quartile plots can invite statistical artifact; directly comparing individual differences in metacognitive precision and task performance with an independent ability measure is more informative.

A participant's difference between estimated percentile and realized percentile is not itself proof of ignorance of their errors. Realized performance contains noise, and conditional selection on low scores can generate a distinctive graph even when self-estimates are unbiased for latent ability.

== Epistemic and semantic relationships ==

The subject belongs to [[Related to::Metacognition|metacognitive measurement]], [[Related to::Cognition|cognition]], [[Related to::Reasoning|judgment]] and [[Related to::Scientific model|model selection]]. The “Disputed” status reflects the explanatory and measurement controversy, not a declaration that all findings in the original paper are invalid.

A useful advanced analysis must state whether it is testing absolute self-assessment, percentile judgments, ability-related error in calibration, or the underlying source of the error. These are not the same research question.

